WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.970
- Breaking news, Crypto-Play
turned out to be a big scam.

00:00:02.970 --> 00:00:04.290
In other news, the sky is blue

00:00:04.290 --> 00:00:06.270
and those clowns in Congress did it again.

00:00:06.270 --> 00:00:07.552
What a bunch of clowns.

00:00:07.552 --> 00:00:10.140
- (chuckles) How's he keep
up with the news like that?

00:00:10.140 --> 00:00:12.720
- Last week, FTX, the second
largest cryptocurrency exchange

00:00:12.720 --> 00:00:14.500
in the world filed for bankruptcy.

00:00:14.500 --> 00:00:15.333
- It's FTX.

00:00:15.333 --> 00:00:17.417
I's a safe and easy
way to get into crypto.

00:00:19.065 --> 00:00:20.730
- Eh, I don't think so.

00:00:20.730 --> 00:00:23.070
- 2022 was a bad year for cryptocurrency,

00:00:23.070 --> 00:00:25.050
but even in this volatile market,

00:00:25.050 --> 00:00:27.750
FTX's sudden implosion
took people by surprise.

00:00:27.750 --> 00:00:30.030
Many saw FTX as the
squeaky clean white knight

00:00:30.030 --> 00:00:31.110
in the crypto world,

00:00:31.110 --> 00:00:32.400
but with FTX in bankruptcy,

00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:35.760
tens of billions of dollars,
AKA real money, missing

00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:37.770
and giant holes in the balance sheet,

00:00:37.770 --> 00:00:39.780
there's going to be hell to pay,

00:00:39.780 --> 00:00:42.570
which is why lawyers are
the real white knights.

00:00:42.570 --> 00:00:44.160
So what did FTX do wrong?

00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:46.500
What laws might they have
broken and who is going to jail?

00:00:46.500 --> 00:00:47.520
Or maybe it would be easier

00:00:47.520 --> 00:00:50.430
to talk about who's not going to jail.

00:00:50.430 --> 00:00:51.263
But hold on, folks.

00:00:51.263 --> 00:00:52.440
We have a lot of ground to cover,

00:00:52.440 --> 00:00:55.200
because there were probably
a lot of crimes broken.

00:00:55.200 --> 00:00:56.970
FTX had over a million users

00:00:56.970 --> 00:00:58.800
and was recently valued at $32 billion.

00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:02.070
The company was founded in
2019 by Sam Bankman-Fried,

00:01:02.070 --> 00:01:03.990
a 30-year-old ostensible wonder kid.

00:01:03.990 --> 00:01:05.490
Bankman-Fried garnered lots of attention

00:01:05.490 --> 00:01:06.900
for having a romantic relationship

00:01:06.900 --> 00:01:08.190
with 10 other crypto people

00:01:08.190 --> 00:01:09.990
and playing League of
Legends during meetings,

00:01:09.990 --> 00:01:14.160
which apparently was a sign
of genius and business acumen.

00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:17.100
And if so, I guess I need
to give my intern a raise.

00:01:17.100 --> 00:01:18.570
But those warning signs didn't stop him

00:01:18.570 --> 00:01:20.010
from securing massive investments

00:01:20.010 --> 00:01:21.750
from some of the biggest
funders in Silicon Valley.

00:01:21.750 --> 00:01:24.810
If anything, it helped
him procure that funding.

00:01:24.810 --> 00:01:25.980
Foreshadowing!

00:01:25.980 --> 00:01:28.680
But Bankman-Fried became the
darling of the crypto industry,

00:01:28.680 --> 00:01:31.290
swooping into rescue
Robinhood and Voyager Digital

00:01:31.290 --> 00:01:32.730
when they were struggling
earlier this year

00:01:32.730 --> 00:01:35.100
and donating millions of
dollars to the Democratic Party.

00:01:35.100 --> 00:01:36.270
And now, surprising no one,

00:01:36.270 --> 00:01:38.670
BlockFi, which was rescued by FTX,

00:01:38.670 --> 00:01:40.410
is now going bankrupt as well.

00:01:40.410 --> 00:01:42.420
Sam Bankman-Fried, or
SBF, as he was known,

00:01:42.420 --> 00:01:44.640
got his start at Jane Street,
a quantitative trading firm

00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:47.460
that focused on arbitrage opportunities

00:01:47.460 --> 00:01:49.680
in capital market flows.

00:01:49.680 --> 00:01:51.090
After that, he started a hedge fund

00:01:51.090 --> 00:01:52.860
called Alameda Research in 2017.

00:01:53.869 --> 00:01:55.530
Bankman-Fried made his first fortune

00:01:55.530 --> 00:01:57.510
by capitalizing on an
arbitrage opportunity

00:01:57.510 --> 00:02:00.060
where he could buy crypto
coins at low prices in the U.S.

00:02:00.060 --> 00:02:02.520
and sell them at much
higher price in Japan,

00:02:02.520 --> 00:02:04.860
and in 2019, Bankman-Fried started FTX,

00:02:04.860 --> 00:02:06.600
a crypto exchange which
set out to accommodate

00:02:06.600 --> 00:02:09.090
the kind of trading that
he was doing at Alameda.

00:02:09.090 --> 00:02:11.760
The exchange made fees
on the buying and selling

00:02:11.760 --> 00:02:14.040
of different assets, some of
which are illegal in the U.S.

00:02:14.040 --> 00:02:15.450
because they were too risky.

00:02:15.450 --> 00:02:17.520
Again, more foreshadowing.
(thunder rumbles)

00:02:17.520 --> 00:02:20.040
But that's one of the reasons
why FTX and other companies

00:02:20.040 --> 00:02:21.510
were incorporated in The Bahamas,

00:02:21.510 --> 00:02:24.660
and FTX and Alameda also spawned
a host of sister companies

00:02:24.660 --> 00:02:26.490
that are involved in the bankruptcy.

00:02:26.490 --> 00:02:29.160
Now, FTX became the second or
third largest crypto exchange

00:02:29.160 --> 00:02:31.770
by volume, and often became
the lender of last resort

00:02:31.770 --> 00:02:33.630
in many failed crypto projects.

00:02:33.630 --> 00:02:37.170
Some called Bankman-Fried the
JP Morgan of the crypto world,

00:02:37.170 --> 00:02:38.190
echoing the way that Morgan

00:02:38.190 --> 00:02:40.470
had scooped up assets at low prices,

00:02:40.470 --> 00:02:42.630
and Bankman-Fried often suggested

00:02:42.630 --> 00:02:45.390
that he was going to buy
Goldman Sachs at one point.

00:02:45.390 --> 00:02:47.730
Alas, that day is never going to come,

00:02:47.730 --> 00:02:49.470
because reports now say that Bankman-Fried

00:02:49.470 --> 00:02:51.780
may have improperly
transferred $10 billion

00:02:51.780 --> 00:02:54.180
in customer money to his
hedge fund, Alameda Research,

00:02:54.180 --> 00:02:57.150
and that at least $1 billion
of that money is missing.

00:02:57.150 --> 00:03:00.000
Hackers made off with over
$400 million in tokens.

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:02.580
Some speculate that this
might have been an inside job.

00:03:02.580 --> 00:03:04.440
And two weeks ago, the
company said that its tokens

00:03:04.440 --> 00:03:06.600
were worth about $5.5 billion,

00:03:06.600 --> 00:03:07.680
but the real value may have been

00:03:07.680 --> 00:03:09.420
only in the hundreds of millions,

00:03:09.420 --> 00:03:12.270
with less than $1
million in liquid assets.

00:03:12.270 --> 00:03:14.760
Bankman-Fried was worth
$30 billion at one point,

00:03:14.760 --> 00:03:17.040
but lost at least 94% of that

00:03:17.040 --> 00:03:19.320
as it was wiped out in a single day.

00:03:19.320 --> 00:03:21.780
And all this has regulators,
courts, and lawyers

00:03:21.780 --> 00:03:25.260
circling FTX's corpse, so
there's already lawsuits

00:03:25.260 --> 00:03:27.960
and it's possible that
everyone is going to jail.

00:03:27.960 --> 00:03:29.670
And part of the reason
is that Bankman-Fried

00:03:29.670 --> 00:03:31.230
allegedly started moving or lending

00:03:31.230 --> 00:03:33.900
billions of dollars from
FTX, customer money,

00:03:33.900 --> 00:03:35.010
to Alameda Research,

00:03:35.010 --> 00:03:37.440
which suffered big losses
through risky trades.

00:03:37.440 --> 00:03:40.350
Alameda put up FTX tokens, known as FTT,

00:03:40.350 --> 00:03:42.240
as collateral for the loans.

00:03:42.240 --> 00:03:43.350
This turned out to be problematic,

00:03:43.350 --> 00:03:46.350
because FTX tokens weren't
widely traded outside of FTX

00:03:46.350 --> 00:03:47.610
and were highly correlated

00:03:47.610 --> 00:03:50.850
with the success or failure of FTX itself.

00:03:50.850 --> 00:03:52.590
Now, we'll talk about
this more in a second,

00:03:52.590 --> 00:03:55.080
but this is part of
the magic beans problem

00:03:55.080 --> 00:03:57.630
that's inherent in a lot of
different crypto projects

00:03:57.630 --> 00:03:58.830
throughout the entire industry.

00:03:58.830 --> 00:04:01.140
And recently, FTX ran
into financial problems

00:04:01.140 --> 00:04:03.210
and started shopping
around for investments.

00:04:03.210 --> 00:04:05.190
Bankman-Fried sent investors a term sheet

00:04:05.190 --> 00:04:06.690
that was a complete mess.

00:04:06.690 --> 00:04:08.850
According to investors
who reviewed the pitch,

00:04:08.850 --> 00:04:11.340
quote, "It contained hastily
arranged balance sheets,

00:04:11.340 --> 00:04:13.890
mostly in unformatted Excel spreadsheets

00:04:13.890 --> 00:04:16.140
that were replete with unformatted typos

00:04:16.140 --> 00:04:18.390
and unnamed pages like Sheet 3.

00:04:18.390 --> 00:04:21.120
No advisors or standard
information was included."

00:04:21.120 --> 00:04:22.920
And because of the sloppy
nature of the balance sheet,

00:04:22.920 --> 00:04:25.380
the data itself was questionable.

00:04:25.380 --> 00:04:27.660
The balance sheet showed that
a lot of Alameda's assets

00:04:27.660 --> 00:04:31.590
consisted of FTT, which is
the same name as FTX's tokens.

00:04:31.590 --> 00:04:33.780
This meant that the tokens
were much less valuable

00:04:33.780 --> 00:04:34.710
than previously thought.

00:04:34.710 --> 00:04:36.180
Matt Levine of Bloomberg explains

00:04:36.180 --> 00:04:37.470
why this is especially notable.

00:04:37.470 --> 00:04:39.480
Quote, "The main attribute is that FTX

00:04:39.480 --> 00:04:41.400
periodically uses a portion of its profits

00:04:41.400 --> 00:04:43.290
to buy back FTT tokens.

00:04:43.290 --> 00:04:46.350
This makes FTT kind of like stock in FTX.

00:04:46.350 --> 00:04:47.820
The higher FTX's profits are,

00:04:47.820 --> 00:04:49.710
the higher the price of FTT will be."

00:04:49.710 --> 00:04:51.210
And as one Twitter user summarized it,

00:04:51.210 --> 00:04:53.580
quote, "I created a
billion shares for my house

00:04:53.580 --> 00:04:55.770
and sold one to my wife for $1,

00:04:55.770 --> 00:04:57.930
valuing my house at $1 billion.

00:04:57.930 --> 00:04:59.580
Give me $500 million please."

00:04:59.580 --> 00:05:00.690
Now, throughout this whole affair,

00:05:00.690 --> 00:05:02.730
FTX's main competitor was Binance.

00:05:02.730 --> 00:05:04.470
Its CEO, Changpeng Zhao,

00:05:04.470 --> 00:05:06.420
started talking trash
about FTT on Twitter,

00:05:06.420 --> 00:05:08.520
saying, quote, "As part of Binance's exit

00:05:08.520 --> 00:05:10.080
from FTX equity last year,

00:05:10.080 --> 00:05:13.770
Binance received roughly
$2.1 billion USD equivalent

00:05:13.770 --> 00:05:16.290
in cash, BUSD and FTT.

00:05:16.290 --> 00:05:18.330
Due to recent revelations
that have come to light,

00:05:18.330 --> 00:05:21.180
we've decided to liquidate any
remaining FTT on our books."

00:05:21.180 --> 00:05:23.280
Now, the FTT tokens were
also the main liquid asset

00:05:23.280 --> 00:05:27.060
FTX had to sell to pay
users making withdrawals,

00:05:27.060 --> 00:05:28.950
so if a bunch of people lost faith in FTX

00:05:28.950 --> 00:05:30.630
and wanted to take all their money out,

00:05:30.630 --> 00:05:32.520
it would have to sell
its own cryptocurrency

00:05:32.520 --> 00:05:33.510
to get the money.

00:05:33.510 --> 00:05:35.790
And if people don't have
faith in your crypto exchange,

00:05:35.790 --> 00:05:36.780
they probably don't have faith

00:05:36.780 --> 00:05:38.100
in your cryptocurrency either,

00:05:38.100 --> 00:05:40.680
so it might not be worth
enough to pay them back.

00:05:40.680 --> 00:05:43.950
And as a result, FTX crashed
upon Zhao's announcement

00:05:43.950 --> 00:05:46.290
that he was selling all of Binance's FTT,

00:05:46.290 --> 00:05:48.660
causing the coin's value to plummet.

00:05:48.660 --> 00:05:51.240
Then there was a bank run
or crypto exchange run,

00:05:51.240 --> 00:05:53.730
in which retail investors
sought to withdraw their funds

00:05:53.730 --> 00:05:55.680
at the same time, though in the aftermath,

00:05:55.680 --> 00:05:57.960
Binance offered to buy FTX International

00:05:57.960 --> 00:05:59.160
as sort of a bailout,

00:05:59.160 --> 00:06:00.990
though Binance later
pulled out of the purchase,

00:06:00.990 --> 00:06:03.330
solidifying the fact
that FTX was belly up.

00:06:03.330 --> 00:06:04.890
This set off a chain reaction

00:06:04.890 --> 00:06:07.680
as the rest of FTX users
tried to pull their money out,

00:06:07.680 --> 00:06:09.300
but FTX didn't have their money,

00:06:09.300 --> 00:06:11.250
or to the extent that
it had made investments,

00:06:11.250 --> 00:06:13.140
those investments were illiquid.

00:06:13.140 --> 00:06:14.790
And Bankman-Fried announced on Twitter

00:06:14.790 --> 00:06:17.190
that it would be halting
non-fiat withdrawals.

00:06:17.190 --> 00:06:19.740
Days later, FTX, Alameda,
and its associated companies

00:06:19.740 --> 00:06:21.270
filed for Chapter 11 bankruptcy.

00:06:21.270 --> 00:06:23.100
Now, Chapter 11 bankruptcy
is sometimes called

00:06:23.100 --> 00:06:24.720
a reorganization bankruptcy,

00:06:24.720 --> 00:06:26.790
and FTX's bankruptcy is complicated

00:06:26.790 --> 00:06:28.740
because it includes
dozens of related entities

00:06:28.740 --> 00:06:29.940
and shell companies.

00:06:29.940 --> 00:06:32.760
Over 20 FTX companies have
bankruptcy petitions so far,

00:06:32.760 --> 00:06:34.260
including Alameda Research.

00:06:34.260 --> 00:06:37.200
The FTX entities filed slap
dash bankruptcy petitions,

00:06:37.200 --> 00:06:39.060
asking the court to
impose an automatic stay

00:06:39.060 --> 00:06:41.010
against the collection
of any outstanding debts,

00:06:41.010 --> 00:06:43.410
but the picture got a little
clearer when FTX filed a motion

00:06:43.410 --> 00:06:44.550
asking if it could file

00:06:44.550 --> 00:06:47.160
a list of its 50 biggest
creditors by Friday.

00:06:47.160 --> 00:06:48.930
In the motion, FTX said it could have

00:06:48.930 --> 00:06:51.990
up to a million creditors,
probably mostly retail people.

00:06:51.990 --> 00:06:54.690
It said, quote, "As set forth
in the debtor's petitions,

00:06:54.690 --> 00:06:58.230
there are over 100,000 creditors
in these Chapter 11 cases.

00:06:58.230 --> 00:07:01.140
In fact, there could be more
than 1 million creditors."

00:07:01.140 --> 00:07:03.480
Many of these creditors
will be FTX customers.

00:07:03.480 --> 00:07:05.760
In other bankruptcy
cases involving crypto,

00:07:05.760 --> 00:07:08.400
customers have been designated
as unsecured creditors,

00:07:08.400 --> 00:07:11.670
which means they will be last
in line for any repayment.

00:07:11.670 --> 00:07:13.470
Now, two days after the
bankruptcy petitions,

00:07:13.470 --> 00:07:14.940
FTX appointed a new CEO

00:07:14.940 --> 00:07:17.910
to see the companies through
bankruptcy, John Ray.

00:07:17.910 --> 00:07:20.610
Ray, who was previously
appointed chairman of Enron

00:07:20.610 --> 00:07:22.950
after its bankruptcy,
stated in his declaration

00:07:22.950 --> 00:07:25.170
in support of FTX's Chapter 11 filing,

00:07:25.170 --> 00:07:27.330
quote, "Never in my career have I seen

00:07:27.330 --> 00:07:29.550
such a complete failure
of corporate controls

00:07:29.550 --> 00:07:31.050
and such a complete absence

00:07:31.050 --> 00:07:32.850
of trustworthy financial information.

00:07:32.850 --> 00:07:34.350
From compromised systems integrity

00:07:34.350 --> 00:07:36.600
and faulty regulatory oversight abroad

00:07:36.600 --> 00:07:38.130
to the concentration of control

00:07:38.130 --> 00:07:40.410
in the hands of a very small
group of inexperienced,

00:07:40.410 --> 00:07:43.530
unsophisticated, and potentially
compromised individuals,

00:07:43.530 --> 00:07:45.690
this situation is unprecedented."

00:07:45.690 --> 00:07:46.980
This isn't a legal observation,

00:07:46.980 --> 00:07:48.510
but that's definitely
not what you want to hear

00:07:48.510 --> 00:07:51.030
from the guy that did
the unwinding of Enron.

00:07:51.030 --> 00:07:53.040
So what else did we learn
from Ray's declaration?

00:07:53.040 --> 00:07:54.780
Well, besides the fact that FTX companies

00:07:54.780 --> 00:07:57.960
had poor to non-existent
corporate controls,

00:07:57.960 --> 00:08:00.960
many of them also gave out
personal loans to their owners.

00:08:00.960 --> 00:08:03.480
Bankman-Fried, for example,
got a $1 billion loan

00:08:03.480 --> 00:08:06.420
from Alameda Research, and
some indications estimate

00:08:06.420 --> 00:08:08.430
that the fair value of all the crypto

00:08:08.430 --> 00:08:11.370
that FTX International holds
is less than a billion dollars,

00:08:11.370 --> 00:08:13.470
far less than Bankman-Fried's evaluation

00:08:13.470 --> 00:08:17.460
that FTX's holdings of FTT were
worth at least 5.5 billion.

00:08:17.460 --> 00:08:20.100
The declaration also alleges
that FTX's financial statements

00:08:20.100 --> 00:08:22.230
don't include customer liabilities.

00:08:22.230 --> 00:08:23.580
Now, I'm not an expert in these things,

00:08:23.580 --> 00:08:26.040
but I don't think that
meets GAP accounting.

00:08:26.040 --> 00:08:28.590
And if it seems like Bankman-Fried
and his polycule members

00:08:28.590 --> 00:08:31.470
didn't know what was going
on, that's probably because,

00:08:31.470 --> 00:08:33.300
quote, "The FTX group did not maintain

00:08:33.300 --> 00:08:35.070
centralized control of its cash.

00:08:35.070 --> 00:08:36.600
Cash management procedural failures

00:08:36.600 --> 00:08:38.520
included the absence of an accurate list

00:08:38.520 --> 00:08:40.620
of bank accounts and account signatories,

00:08:40.620 --> 00:08:43.530
as well as insufficient attention
to the credit worthiness

00:08:43.530 --> 00:08:45.210
of banking partners around the world."

00:08:45.210 --> 00:08:47.730
I know it's hard to believe,
but these crypto people

00:08:47.730 --> 00:08:50.400
might have made some
bad financial decisions.

00:08:50.400 --> 00:08:52.207
Now, furthermore, Ray attests that, quote,

00:08:52.207 --> 00:08:54.420
"In The Bahamas, I understand
that corporate funds

00:08:54.420 --> 00:08:56.370
of the FTX group were
used to purchase homes

00:08:56.370 --> 00:08:58.800
and other personal items
for employees and advisors."

00:08:58.800 --> 00:09:00.120
And while Ray has been pouring over

00:09:00.120 --> 00:09:01.140
the bankruptcy financials,

00:09:01.140 --> 00:09:02.790
Bankman-Fried has spent a lot of his time

00:09:02.790 --> 00:09:05.820
tweeting apologies for his
mistakes and giving interviews,

00:09:05.820 --> 00:09:08.940
which is not a great legal move there.

00:09:08.940 --> 00:09:10.290
And one interview was a text chain

00:09:10.290 --> 00:09:12.960
between Bankman-Fried and a FOX reporter,

00:09:12.960 --> 00:09:15.090
in which he tells her
that his public beliefs

00:09:15.090 --> 00:09:17.460
in effective altruism
were basically just PR.

00:09:17.460 --> 00:09:19.320
He said he didn't mean
to do unethical things,

00:09:19.320 --> 00:09:21.660
but, quote, "Each individual
decision seemed fine,

00:09:21.660 --> 00:09:24.540
and I didn't realize how big
their sum was until the end."

00:09:24.540 --> 00:09:25.500
And the end result is that

00:09:25.500 --> 00:09:27.750
these FTX companies are insolvent,

00:09:27.750 --> 00:09:28.950
and complicating things further,

00:09:28.950 --> 00:09:30.210
there's the second bankruptcy

00:09:30.210 --> 00:09:32.580
filed by the Bahamian FTX Company,

00:09:32.580 --> 00:09:35.130
which claims to be the primary bankruptcy,

00:09:35.130 --> 00:09:37.230
filing for Chapter 15 bankruptcy.

00:09:37.230 --> 00:09:38.940
And in fighting those
jurisdictional claims,

00:09:38.940 --> 00:09:40.920
the original Delaware bankruptcy lawyers

00:09:40.920 --> 00:09:42.810
are claiming that the government
officials in The Bahamas

00:09:42.810 --> 00:09:44.490
are engaging in all kinds of corrupt acts

00:09:44.490 --> 00:09:46.920
and are basically colluding
with Sam Bankman-Fried.

00:09:46.920 --> 00:09:48.270
It's a giant mess.

00:09:48.270 --> 00:09:49.617
But bankruptcy isn't their only problem.

00:09:49.617 --> 00:09:51.420
The the companies will
have to deal with U.S.

00:09:51.420 --> 00:09:53.640
and foreign regulators,
lawsuits from consumers,

00:09:53.640 --> 00:09:55.800
and even criminal enforcement actions.

00:09:55.800 --> 00:09:57.540
So there's little doubt
that the government

00:09:57.540 --> 00:10:00.930
is now investigating, but
there are two U.S. agencies

00:10:00.930 --> 00:10:02.460
that are vying to regulate crypto,

00:10:02.460 --> 00:10:04.740
and probably this case in particular:

00:10:04.740 --> 00:10:07.290
The Securities in Exchange
Commission, the SEC,

00:10:07.290 --> 00:10:09.960
and the Commodity Futures
Trading Commission, or CFTC.

00:10:09.960 --> 00:10:11.100
Now, if crypto is a security,

00:10:11.100 --> 00:10:13.320
then it falls under the
jurisdiction of the SEC,

00:10:13.320 --> 00:10:16.230
but if it's a commodity,
then the CFTC gets involved.

00:10:16.230 --> 00:10:18.030
Now, the CFTC has exclusive jurisdiction

00:10:18.030 --> 00:10:19.860
over accounts, agreements,
and transactions

00:10:19.860 --> 00:10:21.690
involving swaps or contracts of sale

00:10:21.690 --> 00:10:24.120
of a commodity for future delivery.

00:10:24.120 --> 00:10:25.950
The CFTC has asserted jurisdiction

00:10:25.950 --> 00:10:27.330
over fraud and manipulation

00:10:27.330 --> 00:10:29.280
involving spot market transactions

00:10:29.280 --> 00:10:32.430
relating to commodities which
underlie futures or swaps.

00:10:32.430 --> 00:10:34.467
The CFTC first determined that Bitcoin

00:10:34.467 --> 00:10:35.940
and other virtual currencies

00:10:35.940 --> 00:10:38.423
were properly defined as
commodities back in 2015,

00:10:38.423 --> 00:10:42.180
and the CFTC ruled that, "people
who had created a platform

00:10:42.180 --> 00:10:44.250
for the purchase and
sale of Bitcoin options

00:10:44.250 --> 00:10:45.780
were actually operating a facility

00:10:45.780 --> 00:10:48.150
for the trading or processing of swaps

00:10:48.150 --> 00:10:51.000
without being registered as
a swap execution facility

00:10:51.000 --> 00:10:53.220
or designated contract market."

00:10:53.220 --> 00:10:55.470
The CFTC then initiated
an enforcement actions,

00:10:55.470 --> 00:10:58.800
focusing on classic cases
of fraud and Ponzi schemes.

00:10:58.800 --> 00:11:00.360
Now, courts are still deciding

00:11:00.360 --> 00:11:03.000
whether digital tokens
are securities or not.

00:11:03.000 --> 00:11:05.010
They do this according to the HOWEY Test,

00:11:05.010 --> 00:11:07.440
which refers to the
1946 Supreme Court case

00:11:07.440 --> 00:11:09.360
in which the the Supreme
Court set forth a framework

00:11:09.360 --> 00:11:11.700
for deciding whether an
investment contract exists,

00:11:11.700 --> 00:11:14.340
which states, quote, "There
must be an investment of money

00:11:14.340 --> 00:11:16.680
or other assets and a common enterprise

00:11:16.680 --> 00:11:18.420
with the expectation of profit

00:11:18.420 --> 00:11:20.520
to be derived from the efforts of others.

00:11:20.520 --> 00:11:22.590
The absence of one prong will prove fatal

00:11:22.590 --> 00:11:25.650
to a finding that an investment
is an investment contract."

00:11:25.650 --> 00:11:27.810
And the SEC recently stated,
quote, "The main issue

00:11:27.810 --> 00:11:29.970
in analyzing a digital
asset under the HOWEY Test

00:11:29.970 --> 00:11:32.250
is whether the purchaser
has a reasonable expectation

00:11:32.250 --> 00:11:34.470
of profits or other financial returns

00:11:34.470 --> 00:11:36.510
derived from the efforts of others."

00:11:36.510 --> 00:11:37.890
And while this is certainly up in the air,

00:11:37.890 --> 00:11:39.810
I think it's likely that
courts are going to say

00:11:39.810 --> 00:11:43.500
that cryptocurrencies fall under
the category of securities.

00:11:43.500 --> 00:11:45.660
So FTX executives could be prosecuted

00:11:45.660 --> 00:11:47.400
under U.S. securities laws.

00:11:47.400 --> 00:11:48.660
The company may have violated

00:11:48.660 --> 00:11:52.140
the Securities Exchange
Act of 1934, Rule 10-B,

00:11:52.140 --> 00:11:54.750
which makes it illegal to
directly or indirectly use

00:11:54.750 --> 00:11:57.300
any measure to defraud,
make false statements,

00:11:57.300 --> 00:11:58.710
omit relevant information,

00:11:58.710 --> 00:12:00.600
or otherwise conduct business operations

00:12:00.600 --> 00:12:02.340
that would deceive another person

00:12:02.340 --> 00:12:04.110
in the process of conducting transactions

00:12:04.110 --> 00:12:05.730
involving stock and other securities.

00:12:05.730 --> 00:12:07.620
And traditionally, violations of Rule 10-B

00:12:07.620 --> 00:12:09.300
have included insider trading,

00:12:09.300 --> 00:12:11.370
as well as defrauding
investors by hiding losses

00:12:11.370 --> 00:12:13.860
or low revenue with creative
accounting practices.

00:12:13.860 --> 00:12:16.230
And that's not even including
the possible violations

00:12:16.230 --> 00:12:17.970
of myriad different state laws

00:12:17.970 --> 00:12:20.640
for fraud or misappropriation as well.

00:12:20.640 --> 00:12:22.920
And although FTX was
headquartered in The Bahamas,

00:12:22.920 --> 00:12:25.380
eventually, the company sold
products to American consumers,

00:12:25.380 --> 00:12:28.050
so if you're raising assets
from American customers,

00:12:28.050 --> 00:12:28.883
you're placing yourself

00:12:28.883 --> 00:12:31.020
within the jurisdiction of U.S. laws.

00:12:31.020 --> 00:12:34.170
And one of the main issues
that looks just super bad,

00:12:34.170 --> 00:12:35.460
that's a legal term,

00:12:35.460 --> 00:12:38.550
is that FTX loaned customer
money to Alameda Research.

00:12:38.550 --> 00:12:40.710
It's reported that customers
did not know that FTX

00:12:40.710 --> 00:12:43.740
was using their funds to make
financially risky trades.

00:12:43.740 --> 00:12:45.690
When customers sought
to withdraw their money,

00:12:45.690 --> 00:12:47.880
they discovered that their
funds were not available.

00:12:47.880 --> 00:12:50.010
And there's another interesting
wrinkle to the scheme,

00:12:50.010 --> 00:12:53.130
which is that Alameda would
then buy up FTT tokens

00:12:53.130 --> 00:12:54.780
to prop up FTX.

00:12:54.780 --> 00:12:56.190
This makes the entire enterprise

00:12:56.190 --> 00:12:58.410
look more and more like a Ponzi scheme,

00:12:58.410 --> 00:13:01.560
where both FTX and Alameda were
looking out for each other,

00:13:01.560 --> 00:13:03.870
but neither of them were
helping their investors,

00:13:03.870 --> 00:13:05.310
though potentially some
of the liability here

00:13:05.310 --> 00:13:07.590
will depend on the
contracts with customers

00:13:07.590 --> 00:13:09.120
and between the different companies.

00:13:09.120 --> 00:13:11.040
You can't disclaim fraud in a contract,

00:13:11.040 --> 00:13:12.540
but it's possible that
some of the customers

00:13:12.540 --> 00:13:14.820
might have affirmatively
agreed to some of this behavior

00:13:14.820 --> 00:13:17.880
by agreeing to the terms and
conditions of the exchange.

00:13:17.880 --> 00:13:19.860
But does this mean that company executives

00:13:19.860 --> 00:13:21.180
could be charged with crimes?

00:13:21.180 --> 00:13:24.090
Well, let's start with the
most obvious one, wire fraud.

00:13:24.090 --> 00:13:25.140
Wire fraud is a type of fraud

00:13:25.140 --> 00:13:27.510
involving the use of
telecommunications or the internet.

00:13:27.510 --> 00:13:29.850
Wire fraud could include any
form of electronic media,

00:13:29.850 --> 00:13:31.950
including email, social
media, text messages,

00:13:31.950 --> 00:13:34.080
or even good old-fashioned fax machines.

00:13:34.080 --> 00:13:36.210
If the defendant used
interstate telephone calls

00:13:36.210 --> 00:13:37.290
or electronic communications,

00:13:37.290 --> 00:13:38.820
then the government can prosecute.

00:13:38.820 --> 00:13:41.220
And under 18 USC 1343,

00:13:41.220 --> 00:13:43.530
there are four elements
to classic wire fraud:

00:13:43.530 --> 00:13:45.510
One, that the defendant
voluntarily and intentionally

00:13:45.510 --> 00:13:47.040
devised or participated in a scheme

00:13:47.040 --> 00:13:48.750
to defraud another out of money;

00:13:48.750 --> 00:13:51.180
two, that the defendant did
so with the intent to defraud;

00:13:51.180 --> 00:13:53.310
three, that it was reasonably foreseeable

00:13:53.310 --> 00:13:55.200
that interstate wire
communications would be used;

00:13:55.200 --> 00:13:57.090
and four, that interstate
wire communications

00:13:57.090 --> 00:13:58.110
were, in fact, used.

00:13:58.110 --> 00:13:59.400
So, based on what we know,

00:13:59.400 --> 00:14:01.920
could Bankman-Fried be
prosecuted for wire fraud?

00:14:01.920 --> 00:14:03.570
Well, the DOJ probably faces

00:14:03.570 --> 00:14:05.490
two large hurdles to prosecution.

00:14:05.490 --> 00:14:07.020
The first would be jurisdictional.

00:14:07.020 --> 00:14:08.670
FTX and its associated companies

00:14:08.670 --> 00:14:10.050
are incorporated in The Bahamas,

00:14:10.050 --> 00:14:12.690
and up until recently, most
executives lived there,

00:14:12.690 --> 00:14:13.770
and they would argue that the DOJ

00:14:13.770 --> 00:14:15.270
doesn't have jurisdiction
to prosecute them.

00:14:15.270 --> 00:14:17.400
But prosecutors could
overcome this defense

00:14:17.400 --> 00:14:18.810
if they can show that there's a nexus

00:14:18.810 --> 00:14:20.910
between FTX and the United States.

00:14:20.910 --> 00:14:22.230
Prosecutors would say that FTX

00:14:22.230 --> 00:14:23.820
does lots of business in America.

00:14:23.820 --> 00:14:26.160
It has customers in the
U.S., it uses American banks,

00:14:26.160 --> 00:14:28.980
its executives frequently
travel to the U.S. and further

00:14:28.980 --> 00:14:30.480
of the crypto business,

00:14:30.480 --> 00:14:32.655
and it's safe to assume that the DOJ

00:14:32.655 --> 00:14:34.290
could probably clear that
jurisdictional hurdle.

00:14:34.290 --> 00:14:36.540
Now, the second problem
for the DOJ is intent.

00:14:36.540 --> 00:14:39.210
Section 1343 requires
proof that the defendant

00:14:39.210 --> 00:14:41.670
acted with the intent to defraud,

00:14:41.670 --> 00:14:43.800
that is, the intent to deceive and cheat.

00:14:43.800 --> 00:14:46.440
So if Bankman-Fried did a bad
job of managing the company,

00:14:46.440 --> 00:14:47.580
that's not a crime.

00:14:47.580 --> 00:14:48.750
The government would have to prove

00:14:48.750 --> 00:14:50.460
that he did it on purpose.

00:14:50.460 --> 00:14:53.130
Now, Coinbase CEO Brian
Armstrong said that he believed

00:14:53.130 --> 00:14:55.380
that Bankman-Fried did commit fraud.

00:14:55.380 --> 00:14:57.780
Like FTX, Coinbase is a crypto exchange,

00:14:57.780 --> 00:15:00.900
and Armstrong said that he spoke
with both Sam Bankman-Fried

00:15:00.900 --> 00:15:03.300
and Binance CEO Changpeng Zhao,

00:15:03.300 --> 00:15:05.850
who briefly considered buying FTX,

00:15:05.850 --> 00:15:07.230
and during those conversations,

00:15:07.230 --> 00:15:09.570
Armstrong got the impression
that FTX knowingly

00:15:09.570 --> 00:15:12.090
improperly used customer
funds to prop up Alameda.

00:15:12.090 --> 00:15:14.790
- You know, FTX was in a position where

00:15:14.790 --> 00:15:17.070
they had this market maker, Alameda,

00:15:17.070 --> 00:15:19.110
that was investing in risky
things, and that's fine.

00:15:19.110 --> 00:15:20.610
Like, market makers, hedge funds,

00:15:20.610 --> 00:15:22.110
they're designed to take more risk.

00:15:22.110 --> 00:15:25.110
So it seems they had this solvency issue,

00:15:25.110 --> 00:15:26.850
and instead of just letting it blow up,

00:15:26.850 --> 00:15:28.500
Sam basically said, hey,

00:15:28.500 --> 00:15:31.020
we have a bunch of customer
assets over here at FTX,

00:15:31.020 --> 00:15:35.010
or he somehow basically made
a loan from FTX into Alameda

00:15:35.010 --> 00:15:36.480
to try to prop it up.

00:15:36.480 --> 00:15:37.950
I don't know why he did that.

00:15:37.950 --> 00:15:40.350
I mean, that's the moment, in my mind,

00:15:40.350 --> 00:15:43.350
where he crossed the line into
probably committing fraud.

00:15:43.350 --> 00:15:47.580
And I think he probably lied
to users, lied to investors,

00:15:47.580 --> 00:15:49.230
and he went around and tried to bail out

00:15:49.230 --> 00:15:52.350
these different companies,
like Voyager and BlockFi,

00:15:52.350 --> 00:15:54.330
to sort of prop up this thing,

00:15:54.330 --> 00:15:56.490
and maybe he thought he could
trade his way out of it.

00:15:56.490 --> 00:15:58.590
- So FTX customers
believed that their crypto

00:15:58.590 --> 00:15:59.700
was immediately accessible,

00:15:59.700 --> 00:16:02.700
but according to Armstrong,
FTX used customer assets

00:16:02.700 --> 00:16:04.950
to fund the operating
expenses of the company.

00:16:04.950 --> 00:16:07.260
But FTX's terms of service seem

00:16:07.260 --> 00:16:09.390
to clearly prohibit
lending on those assets.

00:16:09.390 --> 00:16:12.330
It says, quote, "None of the
digital assets in your account

00:16:12.330 --> 00:16:14.190
are the property of or shall

00:16:14.190 --> 00:16:16.800
or may be loaned to FTX Trading.

00:16:16.800 --> 00:16:20.190
FTX Trading does not represent
or treat digital assets

00:16:20.190 --> 00:16:23.070
in users' accounts as
belonging to FTX Trading."

00:16:23.070 --> 00:16:24.240
So the government will assess

00:16:24.240 --> 00:16:26.040
all of Bankman-Fried's
statements and the actions

00:16:26.040 --> 00:16:28.950
to figure out if he was
just in over his head

00:16:28.950 --> 00:16:32.100
or whether he knew he
was defrauding customers.

00:16:32.100 --> 00:16:34.620
John Ray accused the companies
of commingling assets

00:16:34.620 --> 00:16:36.600
using company money for personal projects

00:16:36.600 --> 00:16:39.780
and using software to hide
their moves from consumers.

00:16:39.780 --> 00:16:42.330
All of that goes to Bankman-Fried's
intent to commit fraud,

00:16:42.330 --> 00:16:45.030
so I asked former SEC
prosecutor Ron Geffner

00:16:45.030 --> 00:16:46.350
about this whole situation.

00:16:46.350 --> 00:16:48.240
- With regard to FTX,

00:16:48.240 --> 00:16:53.160
there appear to be many
misrepresentations and acts of fraud

00:16:53.160 --> 00:16:54.870
based on the facts that
are currently available.

00:16:54.870 --> 00:16:56.550
The conduct that seems
to be most egregious

00:16:56.550 --> 00:16:59.850
are material misrepresentations
as to user funds

00:16:59.850 --> 00:17:02.580
and where the money was being sent into,

00:17:02.580 --> 00:17:05.160
where it theoretically was being held

00:17:05.160 --> 00:17:07.800
on behalf of consumers and investors.

00:17:07.800 --> 00:17:10.560
It would appear that
corporate governance at FTX

00:17:10.560 --> 00:17:12.780
was, at the very least,
we can all agree, sloppy.

00:17:12.780 --> 00:17:14.700
It may have risen to the level

00:17:14.700 --> 00:17:16.590
where corporate governance was fraudulent,

00:17:16.590 --> 00:17:19.380
and the fact that there
were conflicts of interest,

00:17:19.380 --> 00:17:21.300
there were misrepresentations made

00:17:21.300 --> 00:17:25.530
to both the public and to
both private investors.

00:17:25.530 --> 00:17:27.660
The public and private
investors made decisions

00:17:27.660 --> 00:17:29.850
based on false factual information.

00:17:29.850 --> 00:17:33.930
Whether the principles of FTX
will be prosecuted criminally

00:17:33.930 --> 00:17:36.930
it's gonna depend on whether
the U.S. justice system

00:17:36.930 --> 00:17:39.780
has standing in any case against FTX,

00:17:39.780 --> 00:17:42.780
given much of their
behavior occurred offshore.

00:17:42.780 --> 00:17:47.520
It is out of my wheelhouse, but
based on patterns I've seen,

00:17:47.520 --> 00:17:49.150
even if investors recover

00:17:50.100 --> 00:17:52.620
any kind of remuneration or their assets,

00:17:52.620 --> 00:17:54.930
I have to imagine it would
be pennies on the dollar.

00:17:54.930 --> 00:17:58.170
The takeaway I would have is
pessimism is a good thing,

00:17:58.170 --> 00:17:59.640
no matter what you're investing in.

00:17:59.640 --> 00:18:01.740
It's why do I have this opportunity?

00:18:01.740 --> 00:18:03.390
What is the opportunity?

00:18:03.390 --> 00:18:06.420
What is the upside and
what is the downside risk?

00:18:06.420 --> 00:18:08.760
And in many cases, as human beings,

00:18:08.760 --> 00:18:11.370
when we assess what is
the worst case scenario,

00:18:11.370 --> 00:18:14.490
we rarely ever really
assess what is worst case.

00:18:14.490 --> 00:18:15.990
We usually assess what's a bad case,

00:18:15.990 --> 00:18:17.730
but not what is the worst case.

00:18:17.730 --> 00:18:20.460
And it goes back to, if you
don't understand, don't invest,

00:18:20.460 --> 00:18:22.980
and if you can't afford
to lose it all, then,

00:18:22.980 --> 00:18:25.620
theoretically, don't invest in
these types of opportunities.

00:18:25.620 --> 00:18:27.570
- Now, that's not the
end of the story here,

00:18:27.570 --> 00:18:30.690
because FTX promoted itself and
created an air of legitimacy

00:18:30.690 --> 00:18:33.300
by associating with
celebrities like Tom Brady,

00:18:33.300 --> 00:18:34.920
Giselle Bundchen, Shaquille O'Neal,

00:18:34.920 --> 00:18:36.840
Larry David, and Naomi Osaka,

00:18:36.840 --> 00:18:40.320
and those people have now
been sued by investors

00:18:40.320 --> 00:18:42.720
in a headline-grabbing
class action lawsuit

00:18:42.720 --> 00:18:44.580
Edwin Garrison is the named plaintiff

00:18:44.580 --> 00:18:46.680
who had an FTX yield-bearing account

00:18:46.680 --> 00:18:49.230
which he funded with crypto
assets to earn interest.

00:18:49.230 --> 00:18:51.960
Now, Sam Bankman-Fried is the
lead defendant in this case,

00:18:51.960 --> 00:18:55.020
but investors are also suing
those celebrity promoters,

00:18:55.020 --> 00:18:58.410
and Garrison alleges that,
while FTX lured U.S. investors

00:18:58.410 --> 00:19:00.307
to its yield bearing
accounts, it was, quote,

00:19:00.307 --> 00:19:02.460
"a Ponzi scheme where investor funds

00:19:02.460 --> 00:19:03.930
were shuffled into related entities

00:19:03.930 --> 00:19:05.550
to maintain the appearance of liquidity."

00:19:05.550 --> 00:19:07.500
And while this lawsuit made headlines,

00:19:07.500 --> 00:19:09.480
it's going to be a really uphill battle

00:19:09.480 --> 00:19:11.940
to recover from the celebrity endorsers.

00:19:11.940 --> 00:19:13.800
Plaintiffs allege that defendants violated

00:19:13.800 --> 00:19:15.570
the Florida Investor's Protection Act,

00:19:15.570 --> 00:19:17.490
and the law extends
liability to any director,

00:19:17.490 --> 00:19:20.730
officer, partner, or
agent of or for the seller

00:19:20.730 --> 00:19:23.160
if the director, officer,
partner, or agent

00:19:23.160 --> 00:19:26.550
has personally participated
or aided in making the sale.

00:19:26.550 --> 00:19:28.860
But it's not clear that
a celebrity endorsement

00:19:28.860 --> 00:19:32.040
makes them an agent of the
seller for purposes of this act.

00:19:32.040 --> 00:19:35.790
It's also not clear that
advertising for the FTX Exchange,

00:19:35.790 --> 00:19:38.490
or even, theoretically,
its yield bearing accounts,

00:19:38.490 --> 00:19:41.010
qualifies as a security under the act.

00:19:41.010 --> 00:19:42.210
And as we discussed above,

00:19:42.210 --> 00:19:44.550
it's unclear whether crypto
itself is a security,

00:19:44.550 --> 00:19:46.290
but advertising for the exchange

00:19:46.290 --> 00:19:47.610
rather than a specific security

00:19:47.610 --> 00:19:50.670
adds yet another level of
distraction for the celebrities.

00:19:50.670 --> 00:19:53.250
And similarly, these lawsuits' claims

00:19:53.250 --> 00:19:54.720
for unfair trade practices,

00:19:54.720 --> 00:19:56.940
which relies on an underlying liability,

00:19:56.940 --> 00:20:00.300
or civil conspiracy, which
requires an agreement,

00:20:00.300 --> 00:20:02.490
would be extraordinarily hard to prove

00:20:02.490 --> 00:20:03.960
against the celebrity endorsers.

00:20:03.960 --> 00:20:05.910
Though one wrinkle here
is that both Tom Brady

00:20:05.910 --> 00:20:07.620
and Gisele Bundchen are
reported to have taken

00:20:07.620 --> 00:20:09.570
a huge equity stake in FTX,

00:20:09.570 --> 00:20:11.740
which actually might change the result.

00:20:11.740 --> 00:20:12.573
- [Gisele] What do you think?

00:20:12.573 --> 00:20:13.406
Are you in?

00:20:13.406 --> 00:20:14.590
- You know what?

00:20:14.590 --> 00:20:15.423
I'm in.

00:20:15.423 --> 00:20:16.256
- Now, a lot of people want to know

00:20:16.256 --> 00:20:18.090
whether FTX customers are going
to see any kind of recovery,

00:20:18.090 --> 00:20:21.030
whether through lawsuits
or as unsecured creditors

00:20:21.030 --> 00:20:22.470
in the bankruptcy process.

00:20:22.470 --> 00:20:24.450
And Chapter 11 has been around for forever

00:20:24.450 --> 00:20:26.130
and has a solid process,
but, unfortunately,

00:20:26.130 --> 00:20:28.350
U.S. bankruptcy courts
don't have much experience

00:20:28.350 --> 00:20:30.990
handling bankruptcy claims
of crypto companies.

00:20:30.990 --> 00:20:33.810
Two crypto lending companies,
Celsius and Voyager Digital,

00:20:33.810 --> 00:20:35.880
filed for bankruptcy
protection earlier this year,

00:20:35.880 --> 00:20:38.430
and customer assets are still frozen.

00:20:38.430 --> 00:20:40.410
So it'll take lots of
time, probably years,

00:20:40.410 --> 00:20:42.930
to determine whether FTX
customers can recover anything.

00:20:42.930 --> 00:20:45.930
And in fact, the Mt. Gox
bankruptcy is eight years old

00:20:45.930 --> 00:20:47.700
and is still ongoing.

00:20:47.700 --> 00:20:49.680
And the fact that crypto
is largely unregulated

00:20:49.680 --> 00:20:51.150
means that customers aren't protected

00:20:51.150 --> 00:20:53.010
by the same kinds of safeguards that exist

00:20:53.010 --> 00:20:55.710
when banks and other brokerage firms fail.

